<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/Parakeet.Engine.Python/Parakeet.Engine.Python.csproj" />

    <!--
      Built, never referenced. FakeSidecar is a child process rather than a library: the host starts
      it by path, so what this project needs from it is an executable on disk and not an assembly to
      compile against. ReferenceOutputAssembly="false" says exactly that, and the reference is still
      what makes it build first.

      **It is in Uindosill.slnx, and that is load-bearing rather than tidiness.** A solution build
      unsets the parent's configuration across a ProjectReference, so a referenced project that the
      solution does not list falls back to its own default — Debug. When FakeSidecar was outside the
      solution, `dotnet build Uindosill.slnx -c Release` wrote `tools/FakeSidecar/bin/Debug/`, the
      copy below looked in `bin/Release/` and found nothing, and thirty tests failed on a missing
      executable. `SetConfiguration` metadata does not rescue it; being in the solution does. It
      looked intermittent because anyone who had built this test project directly had a `bin/Release`
      left over from that, which the copy then found.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../tools/FakeSidecar/FakeSidecar.csproj"
                      ReferenceOutputAssembly="false"
                      Private="false" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    Copied beside the tests rather than located by walking up out of the output directory, because
    the walk depends on the configuration and the target framework and would break the day either
    moves. `fake-sidecar/` is where FakeSidecarProcess looks and the only place it looks.
  -->
  <Target Name="CopyFakeSidecar" AfterTargets="Build">
    <ItemGroup>
      <FakeSidecarOutput Include="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)../../tools/FakeSidecar/bin/$(Configuration)/$(TargetFramework)/**/*" />
    </ItemGroup>

    <!--
      Loud rather than silent, because the failure this replaces was neither. A copy that finds
      nothing succeeds, and the first thing anybody hears about it is thirty tests failing on a
      missing file — at which point the question is whether the tests are wrong. Failing here says
      what actually happened, in the build, next to the reason.
    -->
    <Error Condition="'@(FakeSidecarOutput)' == ''"
           Text="Nothing to copy from tools/FakeSidecar/bin/$(Configuration)/$(TargetFramework)/. The stand-in did not build in this configuration, so every test that drives a sidecar would fail on a missing executable. See the ProjectReference above: it is outside the solution, and SetConfiguration is what carries the configuration to it." />

    <Copy SourceFiles="@(FakeSidecarOutput)"
          DestinationFolder="$(OutDir)fake-sidecar/%(RecursiveDir)"
          SkipUnchangedFiles="true" />
  </Target>

</Project>
